WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.167 --> 00:00:01.752
Welcome back to
Academy Dialogues.

00:00:01.752 --> 00:00:03.086
It starts with us.

00:00:03.086 --> 00:00:05.339
Our next discussion is
on reaching gender equity

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.673
in Hollywood.

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.842
We are honored to
have lawyer, academic,

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:12.221
and advocate Anita Hill
moderating today's discussion.

00:00:12.221 --> 00:00:15.807
Joining Anita, Oscar
winning actor Geena Davis.

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:18.769
Sony Pictures animation
executive and Oscar winning

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:21.230
producer, Karen Rupert Toliver.

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.941
Award winning
director Alma Har'el.

00:00:23.941 --> 00:00:27.444
And Academy governor and
composer, Laura Karpman.

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:28.904
Onto you, Anita.

00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:29.988
Thank you, Sean.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.283
And I want to thank
this fabulous panel who

00:00:33.283 --> 00:00:36.870
has agreed to speak with us and
give us some clarity on gender

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:39.081
equality in Hollywood.

00:00:39.081 --> 00:00:43.961
I know that one
doesn't start out

00:00:43.961 --> 00:00:48.548
as a successful
individual in Hollywood,

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:53.512
so I have a question that I want
to start with everyone here.

00:00:53.512 --> 00:00:57.307
At some point in your
lives, I think many of you--

00:00:57.307 --> 00:00:59.643
I suppose many of
you, or all of you--

00:00:59.643 --> 00:01:05.774
were dreaming about a
career in entertainment.

00:01:05.774 --> 00:01:09.278
And as you imagined
that career, I'm

00:01:09.278 --> 00:01:14.449
curious about what you
thought it would be like,

00:01:14.449 --> 00:01:17.411
and why you were drawn to it?

00:01:17.411 --> 00:01:22.708
And finally, just if you could
all just give me briefly,

00:01:22.708 --> 00:01:30.215
how you made that dream of
being in Hollywood a reality?

00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:34.761
And let's see,
where shall I start?

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:40.559
I'll start with you, Geena.

00:01:40.559 --> 00:01:41.768
All right.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:45.188
Well, you know it turns out
that when I was three years old

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:46.815
I announced to my
parents that I wanted

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:50.360
to be an actor in movies.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.988
And I don't know how I
even knew that people

00:01:52.988 --> 00:01:57.743
had jobs at three years old, but
that was it and never changed.

00:01:57.743 --> 00:02:00.245
And majored in
acting in college,

00:02:00.245 --> 00:02:06.209
and was fortunate enough--

00:02:06.209 --> 00:02:08.837
I mean, I did some modeling
and I tried different ways

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:12.883
to get into the business.

00:02:12.883 --> 00:02:17.429
But I eventually got to
audition for the movie Tootsie,

00:02:17.429 --> 00:02:25.020
and I got cast in sort of
a supporting role in that.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.689
And it was stunning
and mind-blowing,

00:02:27.689 --> 00:02:29.107
but at the same
time, well, yeah,

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:32.444
I mean this was what
was supposed to happen.

00:02:32.444 --> 00:02:34.988
So everything kind of
flowed out of that.

00:02:34.988 --> 00:02:38.867
I moved to LA and that was it.

00:02:38.867 --> 00:02:41.244
Was it the same as
what you imagined

00:02:41.244 --> 00:02:44.498
when you were three years old?

00:02:44.498 --> 00:02:50.462
You know, I'm not really sure
why I became so possessed

00:02:50.462 --> 00:02:51.254
with that idea.

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:53.674
I mean, I think in
hindsight, it might

00:02:53.674 --> 00:02:57.844
be that I wanted to be able to
pretend to be somebody else.

00:02:57.844 --> 00:02:59.930
You know, that I was
very insecure and shy,

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.350
and I wanted to live a different
kind of a life than that

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:07.562
and pretend to be other people.

00:03:07.562 --> 00:03:11.024
But it's been-- it really
has been a dream come true.

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:19.658
I mean, acting is the thing
that inspires me and drives me.

00:03:19.658 --> 00:03:21.118
And I love it.

00:03:21.118 --> 00:03:23.120
I really do.

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:24.329
Great.

00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:30.168
Laura, as a composer,
a woman composer,

00:03:30.168 --> 00:03:37.509
I imagine that you don't
see many role-models.

00:03:37.509 --> 00:03:39.803
Or maybe I'm wrong about that.

00:03:39.803 --> 00:03:44.141
But so how did you
come to imagine

00:03:44.141 --> 00:03:49.271
that you would have a career
in entertainment in Hollywood?

00:03:49.271 --> 00:03:51.606
First of all, I can't believe
Anita Hill is asking me

00:03:51.606 --> 00:03:52.732
anything.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:56.111
But let's just move off of that.

00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:59.114
I can best Geena
just by a few years

00:03:59.114 --> 00:04:01.700
on this one, in that
my mother decided

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:05.662
that I would be a composer
when she was pregnant with me.

00:04:05.662 --> 00:04:09.374
So she was a painter,
and she thought

00:04:09.374 --> 00:04:11.501
music was the highest art.

00:04:11.501 --> 00:04:16.173
And for me-- so there
was not a question

00:04:16.173 --> 00:04:18.049
that that would be
something that I would do.

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:21.595
But I thought I was going to
be an East Coast intellectual.

00:04:21.595 --> 00:04:25.390
And I grew up in LA, and
my dad was a cardiologist

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.351
and he had a lot of
celebrities in his practice,

00:04:28.351 --> 00:04:29.769
and a lot of Hollywood people.

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.271
But I just--

00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:33.690
I didn't think that
that was my dream.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:35.567
But I was obsessed with drama.

00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:37.319
I was obsessed with opera.

00:04:37.319 --> 00:04:40.071
I was obsessed with plays,
like I read all of Shakespeare,

00:04:40.071 --> 00:04:42.199
I read all of Eugene
O'Neill's plays.

00:04:42.199 --> 00:04:45.035
And so I was kind of
moving towards it.

00:04:45.035 --> 00:04:46.661
And I went to New
York, and I studied

00:04:46.661 --> 00:04:50.749
with the most intellectual
guy that I could find.

00:04:50.749 --> 00:04:55.629
But sort of interrupted
by that, I went to France

00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:58.673
and studied with a very
famous teacher of composers.

00:04:58.673 --> 00:05:00.675
Her name was Nadia Boulanger.

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:01.510
And she was great.

00:05:01.510 --> 00:05:03.386
She was very old by
the time I got there,

00:05:03.386 --> 00:05:05.305
but she was great
friends with Stravinsky.

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:08.099
And she had trained Aaron
Copland, and Piazzolla

00:05:08.099 --> 00:05:09.893
and a lot of great composers.

00:05:09.893 --> 00:05:12.896
And she was surrounded by women.

00:05:12.896 --> 00:05:14.773
So it wasn't just her.

00:05:14.773 --> 00:05:17.859
It was Louise Talma, who
was an American composer.

00:05:17.859 --> 00:05:20.612
And Mademoiselle, who I don't
know what her last name was.

00:05:20.612 --> 00:05:22.656
I'm not sure she
actually had one.

00:05:22.656 --> 00:05:25.909
But she taught like
really hard caught

00:05:25.909 --> 00:05:29.204
ear training, which is kind of
technical work for composers.

00:05:29.204 --> 00:05:32.749
And I think I realize
now in retrospect

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:35.210
that I had these
models that popped up.

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:38.296
There were never any certainly
no Hollywood composers

00:05:38.296 --> 00:05:41.716
that you knew of, and really
no concert music composers

00:05:41.716 --> 00:05:42.425
that you knew of.

00:05:42.425 --> 00:05:45.178
And now we know of course
they've been around,

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:48.014
but they just have
been made invisible.

00:05:48.014 --> 00:05:51.560
So you know, I
pursued this dream

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:55.313
and then wound up at Sundance as
kind of their new music person.

00:05:55.313 --> 00:05:57.148
And then from then
on, I fell in love

00:05:57.148 --> 00:06:02.696
with scoring the picture and
the rest is a work in progress.

00:06:02.696 --> 00:06:04.239
Let's put it that way.

00:06:04.239 --> 00:06:07.367
Well, it sounds
like for both of you

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.619
it was really a lot of work.

00:06:09.619 --> 00:06:13.415
It wasn't a sort of
instant story of success

00:06:13.415 --> 00:06:20.547
that you read about or
hear about in the movies.

00:06:20.547 --> 00:06:22.716
I think that's the only
place you hear about that.

00:06:22.716 --> 00:06:26.303
You know, the truth is I think
for everybody that it's always

00:06:26.303 --> 00:06:28.513
a long hard road.

00:06:28.513 --> 00:06:33.768
And you pop at certain times
and you don't at other times.

00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:40.317
So Karen, I'm not even sure
that until about a year ago

00:06:40.317 --> 00:06:43.320
I knew what a producer was.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.656
When did you know that
you could be a producer

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:48.742
or would be a producer?

00:06:48.742 --> 00:06:50.368
You know, it's
interesting, because I

00:06:50.368 --> 00:06:52.996
think I've even grown to
a better awareness of what

00:06:52.996 --> 00:06:54.414
a producer is just in life.

00:06:54.414 --> 00:06:56.708
You know, I'm just sort
of making things happen.

00:06:56.708 --> 00:07:00.211
When I was young, I
wanted to be in the arts.

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:04.382
But my parents were scientists,
and so I kind of had that--

00:07:04.382 --> 00:07:06.051
I kind of had a bundle
of contradictions

00:07:06.051 --> 00:07:08.678
that I was very
confident internally,

00:07:08.678 --> 00:07:11.222
but I was really
shy on the outside.

00:07:11.222 --> 00:07:12.932
So I was trying to
kind of figure out--

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.851
I knew I wanted to
be in the industry,

00:07:14.851 --> 00:07:17.646
and I finally got the confidence
to kind of come out here.

00:07:17.646 --> 00:07:20.774
And then looked at the list
of crew names of people

00:07:20.774 --> 00:07:22.233
at the end of the
credits and tried

00:07:22.233 --> 00:07:24.527
to figure out where I fit in.

00:07:24.527 --> 00:07:27.572
And through that
exploration, I realized

00:07:27.572 --> 00:07:30.200
that the putting it together
and the literally producing

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:31.868
and making something
come out of nothing

00:07:31.868 --> 00:07:34.621
was something that was sort
of natural to my personality.

00:07:34.621 --> 00:07:38.958
And did it mostly as a
studio exec for 20 years

00:07:38.958 --> 00:07:40.752
until I got the chance
to do this short.

00:07:40.752 --> 00:07:42.962
And I realized that
this really was

00:07:42.962 --> 00:07:45.882
what I was sort of destined--
at least in my mind--

00:07:45.882 --> 00:07:48.593
to do is to sort of make
something appear out

00:07:48.593 --> 00:07:50.887
of nothing against all odds.

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:53.431
That really kind
of, to me, became

00:07:53.431 --> 00:07:55.558
what it meant to be a producer.

00:07:55.558 --> 00:07:58.812
And I think every day I become
more sort of aware of what that

00:07:58.812 --> 00:08:01.773
means, and sort of how to
sort of hone that craft.

00:08:01.773 --> 00:08:06.069
Well, we're going to talk
about your short in a bit,

00:08:06.069 --> 00:08:08.905
so just hold on to that thought.

00:08:08.905 --> 00:08:12.283
But I want to ask Alma--

00:08:12.283 --> 00:08:18.039
Alma, when I read your bio
I think of you as an artist.

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:25.714
And I don't know if that's
just my interpretation,

00:08:25.714 --> 00:08:30.051
but what led you from
a career in advertising

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:35.765
to a career in entertainment?

00:08:35.765 --> 00:08:37.100
I kind of missed what you said--

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.310
you see me as an expat?

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:40.979
I wasn't sure what you said?

00:08:40.979 --> 00:08:42.230
As an artist.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.275
I mean, that's what your
work brings to mind.

00:08:45.275 --> 00:08:49.738
Someone who is really
truly an artist.

00:08:49.738 --> 00:08:51.531
Thank you.

00:08:51.531 --> 00:08:53.283
Well, I'm also an immigrant.

00:08:53.283 --> 00:08:57.912
So I was born in Tel Aviv,
and I was raised there.

00:08:57.912 --> 00:08:59.956
I really only came
here at the age of 30,

00:08:59.956 --> 00:09:02.667
and it wasn't because I
was pursuing a career.

00:09:02.667 --> 00:09:04.753
It was because I was
pursuing my heart.

00:09:04.753 --> 00:09:09.090
I was in love with a director
who lived here in Los Angeles

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:10.967
by the name of Boaz Yakin.

00:09:10.967 --> 00:09:15.221
And I was already a
director in Israel.

00:09:15.221 --> 00:09:20.685
But I've encountered the same
challenges of gender inequality

00:09:20.685 --> 00:09:24.773
obviously over there, too, as
many women do around the world.

00:09:24.773 --> 00:09:32.655
And it seemed like just every
step had so many kind of levels

00:09:32.655 --> 00:09:34.074
to it.

00:09:34.074 --> 00:09:36.659
It's not that I didn't want
to make films right away,

00:09:36.659 --> 00:09:40.038
but I just had no example of how
to do that and no opportunity

00:09:40.038 --> 00:09:42.165
to do that.

00:09:42.165 --> 00:09:45.210
I didn't grow up with
a family of artists.

00:09:45.210 --> 00:09:49.881
My parents were very
much like blue collar,

00:09:49.881 --> 00:09:53.301
I would say creative people,
but definitely their jobs

00:09:53.301 --> 00:09:54.344
weren't creative.

00:09:54.344 --> 00:09:57.305
And they were just
kind of getting

00:09:57.305 --> 00:10:00.183
by month to month paying
rent and things like that.

00:10:00.183 --> 00:10:02.894
And were very busy with
that and with issues

00:10:02.894 --> 00:10:05.980
of addiction and alcoholism
and things like that.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.233
So really my life was
much more shaped by that.

00:10:08.233 --> 00:10:11.569
And I think I found filmmaking
and expression in general

00:10:11.569 --> 00:10:14.364
as just a form of--

00:10:14.364 --> 00:10:15.949
it was just like a lifeboat.

00:10:15.949 --> 00:10:18.493
It wasn't really that I
had a plan and I was like,

00:10:18.493 --> 00:10:20.954
I'm going to do this and then
I'm going to go to Hollywood,

00:10:20.954 --> 00:10:22.080
or I'm going to be that.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:25.959
It was just how do I say
something or find a way

00:10:25.959 --> 00:10:31.089
to get a sense of self that
would allow me to stop feeling

00:10:31.089 --> 00:10:34.717
like I'm sinking all the
time into the same patterns

00:10:34.717 --> 00:10:35.885
that I grew up with?

00:10:35.885 --> 00:10:42.851
So I guess I started with just
video art and music videos.

00:10:42.851 --> 00:10:44.853
And when I came here,
I did music videos

00:10:44.853 --> 00:10:46.771
for probably almost 10
years or eight years,

00:10:46.771 --> 00:10:49.732
and couldn't even
get to commercials.

00:10:49.732 --> 00:10:53.528
And at a certain point, I
just sort of took a camera

00:10:53.528 --> 00:10:56.781
and went to the Salton Sea
in California in the desert

00:10:56.781 --> 00:10:59.659
and directed a documentary
by myself with no crew.

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:01.870
And it won the
Tribeca Film Festival.

00:11:01.870 --> 00:11:04.789
And after that, I
suddenly got opportunities

00:11:04.789 --> 00:11:07.041
to direct commercials.

00:11:07.041 --> 00:11:12.797
And from there to also meet
other artists and filmmakers

00:11:12.797 --> 00:11:13.464
and actors.

00:11:13.464 --> 00:11:16.384
Like Shia LaBeouf, who saw
my documentary and admired it

00:11:16.384 --> 00:11:19.596
and reached out
to me very kindly.

00:11:19.596 --> 00:11:21.931
So it really kind of
opened up everything

00:11:21.931 --> 00:11:24.017
when I just kind of took
it into my own hands

00:11:24.017 --> 00:11:27.061
and made something that
people can see and appreciate.

00:11:27.061 --> 00:11:29.230
But prior to that,
it was just years

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:33.234
of working on I guess
low budget music videos

00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:39.741
and having not much of a future
that I could see, you know?

00:11:39.741 --> 00:11:43.620
Well, it does sound to me like
from hearing everyone of you

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:48.124
that you have found
yourself, and that you're

00:11:48.124 --> 00:11:52.128
in a place where you
understand your craft

00:11:52.128 --> 00:11:54.839
and your purpose with your work.

00:11:54.839 --> 00:12:01.220
So I just want to ask you at
this point in your career,

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:05.099
how do you want--

00:12:05.099 --> 00:12:08.811
what do you want your
work to represent?

00:12:08.811 --> 00:12:12.649
How do you want the
world to see your work?

00:12:12.649 --> 00:12:17.111
And we can start with you,
Alma, because something you said

00:12:17.111 --> 00:12:22.325
prompted me to think about that.

00:12:22.325 --> 00:12:31.209
I guess I'm really keen on,
in general as an artist,

00:12:31.209 --> 00:12:35.338
I guess finding your own voice
and doing something in a way

00:12:35.338 --> 00:12:40.969
that only you can do has always
been sort of my tendency.

00:12:40.969 --> 00:12:43.012
Or you could say it's a
dream, but it's also just

00:12:43.012 --> 00:12:45.014
like a natural discourse.

00:12:45.014 --> 00:12:47.350
Where I almost--

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:49.560
I'd say I'll prefer
to do something

00:12:49.560 --> 00:12:54.065
that only I can do than to
make the best whatever, right?

00:12:54.065 --> 00:12:56.609
Like a masterpiece.

00:12:56.609 --> 00:13:00.905
Like I just really want to
find my voice and the words

00:13:00.905 --> 00:13:02.949
to say exactly what
you want to say.

00:13:02.949 --> 00:13:07.286
And that's the joy of really
expressing your connection

00:13:07.286 --> 00:13:10.790
to life and your existence,
and your relationship

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:11.749
with this world.

00:13:11.749 --> 00:13:15.086
And I am also very
drawn, obviously,

00:13:15.086 --> 00:13:17.547
from just because
of how I grew up

00:13:17.547 --> 00:13:21.301
and where I'm from, to
people that are flawed.

00:13:21.301 --> 00:13:24.929
People that are
dealing with hardship,

00:13:24.929 --> 00:13:28.266
with difficulties, whether
it's psychological, emotional,

00:13:28.266 --> 00:13:30.810
or racial, or financial.

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:32.854
All of those things.

00:13:32.854 --> 00:13:39.110
Immigrants, people that weren't
really born with a plan.

00:13:39.110 --> 00:13:42.405
Weren't really born with a path.

00:13:42.405 --> 00:13:47.452
And I think womenism and
womanhood and feminism

00:13:47.452 --> 00:13:51.205
includes all of that, because
so many of us as women

00:13:51.205 --> 00:13:57.545
have to really make our
path, and not just find it.

00:13:57.545 --> 00:13:58.629
As opposed to finding it--

00:13:58.629 --> 00:13:59.881
sometimes we have to make it.

00:13:59.881 --> 00:14:03.843
So I guess in my work,
I guess I do find

00:14:03.843 --> 00:14:07.388
myself living in that world.

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:11.100
But I'm also really extremely
interested in the subconscious

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.603
and dreams and imagination.

00:14:13.603 --> 00:14:15.271
So it's really in
the intersection

00:14:15.271 --> 00:14:19.650
of all of those things I think
that I find myself operating.

00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:22.653
And I just find
sometimes that when

00:14:22.653 --> 00:14:26.032
we talk about gender equality,
that there has been somewhat

00:14:26.032 --> 00:14:29.660
of a limiting expectation when
we now talk to women directors

00:14:29.660 --> 00:14:35.249
to represent a certain
identity for women,

00:14:35.249 --> 00:14:39.712
whether it's like only
writing or directing things

00:14:39.712 --> 00:14:43.257
that have a certain kind of role
models and things like that.

00:14:43.257 --> 00:14:47.512
Even though obviously I couldn't
fight harder and agree more

00:14:47.512 --> 00:14:49.722
that we need great
role models on screen

00:14:49.722 --> 00:14:52.225
and that it's life
changing for girls,

00:14:52.225 --> 00:14:55.311
I also want to see flawed women.

00:14:55.311 --> 00:14:59.774
I also want to see women
directing men's stories.

00:14:59.774 --> 00:15:01.025
I want to see--

00:15:01.025 --> 00:15:06.447
I want to step out of that box
of the expectation of women

00:15:06.447 --> 00:15:10.660
only being hired
for certain stories.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:14.622
Anybody else have that
pressure, feel the pressure

00:15:14.622 --> 00:15:19.460
to represent all
womankind in your work?

00:15:19.460 --> 00:15:21.879
Or have you really
thought independently--

00:15:21.879 --> 00:15:24.423
I'm sure you're all
independent thinkers.

00:15:24.423 --> 00:15:26.300
Have you thought
about what you want

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:30.596
to represent in your work
outside of that pressure

00:15:30.596 --> 00:15:32.932
when you have that
kind of freedom?

00:15:32.932 --> 00:15:36.102
Alma, something you said
really resonated with me

00:15:36.102 --> 00:15:39.730
about the whole idea of
role models and everything.

00:15:39.730 --> 00:15:45.319
Which is that when Thelma
and Louise came out--

00:15:45.319 --> 00:15:47.446
Yes!

00:15:47.446 --> 00:15:51.534
The reaction of women that I met
who recognized me on the street

00:15:51.534 --> 00:15:56.664
or whatever was so different
from anything I'd experienced.

00:15:56.664 --> 00:16:00.084
And they were so passionate
about what it meant to them

00:16:00.084 --> 00:16:01.210
and how it impacted them.

00:16:01.210 --> 00:16:04.630
And it made me decide--

00:16:04.630 --> 00:16:06.674
it made me realize
that we give women

00:16:06.674 --> 00:16:12.013
so few opportunities to come
out of a movie feeling inspired

00:16:12.013 --> 00:16:14.098
by the female characters.

00:16:14.098 --> 00:16:16.309
And men, almost every
movie that comes out,

00:16:16.309 --> 00:16:19.020
they can identify with
the male character

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:20.396
and have a vicarious experience.

00:16:20.396 --> 00:16:21.939
But it's so rare for women.

00:16:21.939 --> 00:16:25.526
So from then on,
I decided that I

00:16:25.526 --> 00:16:29.530
wanted to choose roles with the
women in the audience in mind.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:32.116
What are they going to
think about my character?

00:16:32.116 --> 00:16:33.659
How is it going
to make them feel?

00:16:33.659 --> 00:16:38.372
And it was very specifically
not about finding role models,

00:16:38.372 --> 00:16:39.332
because I'm with you--

00:16:39.332 --> 00:16:42.376
I think role model, this whole
idea that female characters

00:16:42.376 --> 00:16:45.546
have to be role models
is very limiting.

00:16:45.546 --> 00:16:50.218
Flawed, messed up characters,
just like people--

00:16:50.218 --> 00:16:54.680
women need to be colorful and
interesting and complicated

00:16:54.680 --> 00:16:55.848
characters.

00:16:55.848 --> 00:16:59.352
But something I do
look for is women

00:16:59.352 --> 00:17:02.146
who are in charge
of their own fate,

00:17:02.146 --> 00:17:06.234
or who can become in
charge of their own fate.

00:17:06.234 --> 00:17:08.110
And for good or bad, you know.

00:17:08.110 --> 00:17:10.029
Make mistakes, or
whatever, but at least

00:17:10.029 --> 00:17:11.739
you're driving your own car.

00:17:11.739 --> 00:17:16.077
And might be off
the cliff, but--

00:17:16.077 --> 00:17:20.039
But that was quite
an awakening for me,

00:17:20.039 --> 00:17:24.293
to think about
how we really have

00:17:24.293 --> 00:17:25.920
to increase the
opportunities for women

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:30.841
to live vicariously
through characters.

00:17:30.841 --> 00:17:35.304
Well, God help us if flawed
people can't be role models.

00:17:35.304 --> 00:17:39.517
I don't think there
would be any role models.

00:17:39.517 --> 00:17:44.855
So I want to hear also
from you, Laura and Karen.

00:17:44.855 --> 00:17:48.484
At this point in your
lives, you done so much

00:17:48.484 --> 00:17:51.070
and you've achieved so much.

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:55.825
What do you want the world to
see when they see your work?

00:17:55.825 --> 00:17:59.829
You know, I'll answer
it in an odd way,

00:17:59.829 --> 00:18:03.374
and in relationship to something
I've just finished working on,

00:18:03.374 --> 00:18:08.170
which is a show for HBO
called Lovecraft Country.

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.881
And in Lovecraft Country--

00:18:10.881 --> 00:18:13.426
and I'll try not to
give anything away--

00:18:13.426 --> 00:18:21.517
these women emerge in these
powerful, powerful positions.

00:18:21.517 --> 00:18:24.979
And it has been my
honor to score them

00:18:24.979 --> 00:18:30.067
with music that's almost
appropriated from a genre that

00:18:30.067 --> 00:18:33.029
has been typically male.

00:18:33.029 --> 00:18:38.117
And so it's giving
these Black women

00:18:38.117 --> 00:18:42.330
what has been perceived of
throughout film scoring history

00:18:42.330 --> 00:18:45.499
as white male superhero music.

00:18:45.499 --> 00:18:47.752
And to have a woman--
me-- and a Black man--

00:18:47.752 --> 00:18:50.880
Rafael Saadiq--
create this music

00:18:50.880 --> 00:18:54.050
for these strong
female characters

00:18:54.050 --> 00:18:58.679
has been a crazy and really,
really beautiful experience.

00:18:58.679 --> 00:19:03.100
And in many ways, it's
playing into genre rather than

00:19:03.100 --> 00:19:04.060
away from genre.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:06.937
So that it's falling
on a lot of the tropes

00:19:06.937 --> 00:19:11.484
that we've seen in other kinds
of films that are, as I said,

00:19:11.484 --> 00:19:12.860
traditionally male.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:14.945
And for me it's
been fun, because I

00:19:14.945 --> 00:19:18.991
think for really one of
the few times in my career

00:19:18.991 --> 00:19:21.952
I've written big movie music.

00:19:21.952 --> 00:19:25.039
Big, almost old
fashioned, movie music.

00:19:25.039 --> 00:19:27.208
And it's been a real pleasure.

00:19:27.208 --> 00:19:30.169
So I mean for me in my life, I
do a lot of different things.

00:19:30.169 --> 00:19:33.297
I have concert music,
which is really

00:19:33.297 --> 00:19:38.719
kind of a direct vision
of my own personal voice

00:19:38.719 --> 00:19:39.929
as a composer.

00:19:39.929 --> 00:19:43.099
As a film composer,
it is necessary for me

00:19:43.099 --> 00:19:46.477
to take that vision
and take my personality

00:19:46.477 --> 00:19:51.357
and do it in service of
great directors, like Alma.

00:19:51.357 --> 00:19:55.444
But in this particular case,
the show runner, Misha Green,

00:19:55.444 --> 00:20:00.658
who is a genius, wanted us
to play with these genres.

00:20:00.658 --> 00:20:06.205
And that has been, I think,
deeply moving and empowering.

00:20:06.205 --> 00:20:07.790
Well, when you talk
about empowerment,

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:10.000
that really is what we're
talking about with gender

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:12.586
equity, isn't it?

00:20:12.586 --> 00:20:17.341
Karen, you're both a
studio executive as well

00:20:17.341 --> 00:20:20.177
now as producer
of creative work.

00:20:24.181 --> 00:20:26.100
You can choose either
one, either role--

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:29.562
what do you want your
work to represent?

00:20:29.562 --> 00:20:31.939
Well, I got inspired
to come to the industry

00:20:31.939 --> 00:20:33.983
because I wanted to
make seminal movies.

00:20:33.983 --> 00:20:36.152
And it's the Thelma
and Louises and things

00:20:36.152 --> 00:20:39.196
that really just stuck
with you that changed you.

00:20:39.196 --> 00:20:43.075
Like on a cellular level,
these movies kind of changed me

00:20:43.075 --> 00:20:44.743
as I grew up.

00:20:44.743 --> 00:20:46.454
And I wanted to
be a part of that.

00:20:46.454 --> 00:20:50.166
I wanted to make movies that
really landed in some way.

00:20:50.166 --> 00:20:52.501
And a lot of them moved
the needle in some way

00:20:52.501 --> 00:20:55.963
so that they sort of
opened up a perception,

00:20:55.963 --> 00:20:58.132
or changed just what I
thought was possible.

00:20:58.132 --> 00:21:00.968
Changed my mind about
things, changed my heart.

00:21:00.968 --> 00:21:04.722
And so those are the movies that
I really want to keep pushing.

00:21:04.722 --> 00:21:09.310
So looking at, like you said,
the traditional male roles--

00:21:09.310 --> 00:21:11.103
and thank you, Geena,
for all of your work.

00:21:11.103 --> 00:21:12.521
I know you're going
to talk about.

00:21:12.521 --> 00:21:14.106
But just to really
go, OK, this would

00:21:14.106 --> 00:21:15.858
have been done for a
male, this would have

00:21:15.858 --> 00:21:17.151
been done for someone white.

00:21:17.151 --> 00:21:21.030
What would it look like for
a person of color or a woman

00:21:21.030 --> 00:21:21.989
to inhabit those?

00:21:21.989 --> 00:21:25.034
So that they can be as
varied and have the spectrum,

00:21:25.034 --> 00:21:26.785
and keep pushing
what that looks like.

00:21:26.785 --> 00:21:29.914
And every time, if
it's already been done,

00:21:29.914 --> 00:21:31.457
that's not where I want to go.

00:21:31.457 --> 00:21:32.791
Although I love, Laura,
what you're saying

00:21:32.791 --> 00:21:34.502
is like, OK, it's been
done for men before,

00:21:34.502 --> 00:21:36.253
but how do we show it
with a Black female?

00:21:36.253 --> 00:21:37.838
Or how do we really push it?

00:21:37.838 --> 00:21:39.215
And it opens our minds.

00:21:39.215 --> 00:21:41.675
And I think every time we
do movies like that, I just

00:21:41.675 --> 00:21:43.177
want to be part of
that conversation

00:21:43.177 --> 00:21:45.554
and part of that process.

00:21:45.554 --> 00:21:47.723
A couple of you have
alluded to the fact

00:21:47.723 --> 00:21:54.939
that you have experienced
gender inequality in this career

00:21:54.939 --> 00:21:56.190
that you've had.

00:21:56.190 --> 00:22:00.736
And so this is the
discussion of the day.

00:22:00.736 --> 00:22:05.616
So let me just ask you a few
questions along those lines,

00:22:05.616 --> 00:22:09.662
too, because I
think people really

00:22:09.662 --> 00:22:14.458
do think that the fairy tale
is true-- that you get here

00:22:14.458 --> 00:22:20.214
and you become successful,
and the path is just

00:22:20.214 --> 00:22:22.299
a bed of roses.

00:22:22.299 --> 00:22:23.634
I mixed metaphors.

00:22:23.634 --> 00:22:26.804
You know, that
it's an easy path.

00:22:26.804 --> 00:22:28.722
But it does sound like
you had a lot of work,

00:22:28.722 --> 00:22:30.891
and I'm sure you've met
a lot of challenges.

00:22:30.891 --> 00:22:34.979
Now, three of you have
started your own organizations

00:22:34.979 --> 00:22:40.192
in response to some of the
challenges that you've seen.

00:22:40.192 --> 00:22:41.902
And Karen, of course,
you were talking

00:22:41.902 --> 00:22:45.281
about working within a
role of a studio exec

00:22:45.281 --> 00:22:49.410
to address some of the gender
inequities that you've seen.

00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:55.749
But I'd like to hear
from each of you

00:22:55.749 --> 00:22:59.420
what was it maybe
that sparked you

00:22:59.420 --> 00:23:02.798
to start your organizations?

00:23:02.798 --> 00:23:07.303
And Geena, we know
about the number of ways

00:23:07.303 --> 00:23:14.560
that gender imbalances occur
in the industry on film.

00:23:14.560 --> 00:23:19.231
How did you then decide that you
wanted to do something about it

00:23:19.231 --> 00:23:22.568
and start your institute?

00:23:22.568 --> 00:23:29.491
Well, so people maybe have
heard this story before,

00:23:29.491 --> 00:23:31.201
but when my daughter
was a toddler

00:23:31.201 --> 00:23:34.079
and I started showing
her little kids shows

00:23:34.079 --> 00:23:37.791
and preschool
things, I immediately

00:23:37.791 --> 00:23:40.461
noticed that there seemed
to be far more male

00:23:40.461 --> 00:23:44.548
characters than female
characters in the 21st century

00:23:44.548 --> 00:23:46.133
in what we're showing to kids.

00:23:46.133 --> 00:23:48.510
And I didn't intend
in the beginning

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:50.220
to make it my life's
mission or anything,

00:23:50.220 --> 00:23:54.683
but I couldn't find anybody
else who said, I know right?

00:23:54.683 --> 00:23:55.851
I noticed, too.

00:23:55.851 --> 00:23:58.520
And in fact, when I talked
to people in the industry,

00:23:58.520 --> 00:23:59.480
they all said, no, no.

00:23:59.480 --> 00:24:00.773
But that's not true anymore.

00:24:00.773 --> 00:24:01.523
That's been fixed.

00:24:01.523 --> 00:24:03.067
We fixed it.

00:24:03.067 --> 00:24:04.735
And I thought, OK.

00:24:04.735 --> 00:24:06.445
Nobody sees what
I'm talking about,

00:24:06.445 --> 00:24:10.616
and I think this is
profoundly important to fix.

00:24:10.616 --> 00:24:13.827
And so I decided I
wanted to get the data.

00:24:13.827 --> 00:24:16.914
And the purpose of the
data was very specific,

00:24:16.914 --> 00:24:19.375
that rather than
trying to educate

00:24:19.375 --> 00:24:21.585
the populace about
this problem, I

00:24:21.585 --> 00:24:24.546
wanted to go directly to
the creators very privately

00:24:24.546 --> 00:24:26.423
and say, hey, I don't
think you realize this,

00:24:26.423 --> 00:24:27.549
but what do you think?

00:24:27.549 --> 00:24:31.303
And so that's what we've been
doing for about 15 years.

00:24:31.303 --> 00:24:38.310
And it's working directly
in a non-judgemental

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:42.314
and in a supportive
collaborative way to say,

00:24:42.314 --> 00:24:46.110
I don't think you realize this,
but how about we improve it?

00:24:46.110 --> 00:24:47.444
And your focus was children?

00:24:47.444 --> 00:24:49.029
She's being very modest.

00:24:49.029 --> 00:24:51.573
Yeah, it's brought huge change.

00:24:51.573 --> 00:24:55.244
And those PG-13
especially in movies

00:24:55.244 --> 00:24:58.288
that started to inspire you,
it's almost reached parity.

00:24:58.288 --> 00:25:01.750
We're still suffering,
but the children movies

00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:06.338
have become so much better
with representation.

00:25:06.338 --> 00:25:08.757
And I honestly
believe that it goes--

00:25:08.757 --> 00:25:14.430
your influence has gone
beyond the movie industry,

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:15.180
well beyond.

00:25:15.180 --> 00:25:18.225
I mean, the information
and data is so rich

00:25:18.225 --> 00:25:20.436
that it's informed
a lot of the way we

00:25:20.436 --> 00:25:23.397
think about work and gender.

00:25:23.397 --> 00:25:29.778
And Karen, as I was thinking
about Geena and her work

00:25:29.778 --> 00:25:33.240
and what sparked her work
was really her daughter,

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:36.034
and what was her
daughter seeing?

00:25:36.034 --> 00:25:39.329
And I'm thinking
about Hair Love.

00:25:39.329 --> 00:25:44.042
And as I'm watching, you've
got these three characters.

00:25:44.042 --> 00:25:46.670
I don't know how
you captured them--

00:25:46.670 --> 00:25:49.339
how they were captured
in such a short film.

00:25:49.339 --> 00:25:52.968
But you got a sense of all
three of the characters.

00:25:52.968 --> 00:25:56.430
But did you write Hair
Love for children?

00:25:56.430 --> 00:26:01.769
Did you write it
for your child self?

00:26:01.769 --> 00:26:04.646
I mean, I saw my
child hair story

00:26:04.646 --> 00:26:09.526
of being hair shamed
by a white classmate.

00:26:09.526 --> 00:26:14.698
I saw that in Hair Love.

00:26:14.698 --> 00:26:19.787
So what did you--

00:26:19.787 --> 00:26:27.002
how were you thinking about
Hair Love in terms of issues

00:26:27.002 --> 00:26:33.801
that children are facing and
getting wrong messages about?

00:26:33.801 --> 00:26:34.343
Sure.

00:26:34.343 --> 00:26:37.054
I mean, Hair Love
was such a blessing.

00:26:37.054 --> 00:26:38.847
And it came to me--

00:26:38.847 --> 00:26:42.226
Matthew Cherry, who kind of
got the idea for it-- and it

00:26:42.226 --> 00:26:44.728
still surprises
me how many layers

00:26:44.728 --> 00:26:49.316
it sort of touches in terms
of my growth as a Black woman.

00:26:49.316 --> 00:26:51.443
You know, just so
many things we kind of

00:26:51.443 --> 00:26:53.403
poured our hearts into it.

00:26:53.403 --> 00:26:57.574
Matthew was seeing online
dads posting about them

00:26:57.574 --> 00:26:59.243
doing their daughter's hair.

00:26:59.243 --> 00:27:02.746
And the funny thing is-- this
goes to sort of the gender

00:27:02.746 --> 00:27:06.041
bias-- is that when women
do their daughter's hair,

00:27:06.041 --> 00:27:07.543
that's just a Monday.

00:27:07.543 --> 00:27:09.711
But when a dad does
a daughter's hair,

00:27:09.711 --> 00:27:11.630
you know it's like, oh
my God, praise the guy.

00:27:11.630 --> 00:27:13.340
He did the hair.

00:27:13.340 --> 00:27:15.092
And the truth is,
a lot of dads are

00:27:15.092 --> 00:27:17.302
doing their daughter's hair.

00:27:17.302 --> 00:27:20.013
And so part of what
Matthew wanted to show

00:27:20.013 --> 00:27:21.390
is just to normalize that.

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:24.810
So that you really-- and I
think it's really important just

00:27:24.810 --> 00:27:27.938
to showcase that, and show
that you can have a man,

00:27:27.938 --> 00:27:33.360
a Black man, that fits the
description of tattoos and all

00:27:33.360 --> 00:27:36.029
those things that you may
perceive him a certain way,

00:27:36.029 --> 00:27:37.447
but you're really
showing that he

00:27:37.447 --> 00:27:40.576
can take on sort of
nurturing parental roles just

00:27:40.576 --> 00:27:41.201
like the woman.

00:27:41.201 --> 00:27:43.412
And so that was really
one of the first ways

00:27:43.412 --> 00:27:44.288
that we came into it.

00:27:44.288 --> 00:27:48.125
And for me, having two boys,
that was a big part of it.

00:27:48.125 --> 00:27:51.712
But then as you said, the
hair was a whole other level.

00:27:51.712 --> 00:27:54.339
This is the first time after 20
years in the animation business

00:27:54.339 --> 00:27:56.675
that I was sitting in
a room brainstorming

00:27:56.675 --> 00:27:59.469
with other people of color
about our experiences,

00:27:59.469 --> 00:28:01.263
and how we could put
all of that in us.

00:28:01.263 --> 00:28:04.474
And it absolutely took us
all back to our childhood.

00:28:04.474 --> 00:28:05.976
And you know, me
growing up in Texas

00:28:05.976 --> 00:28:08.896
and being the only Black
girl in my class for years.

00:28:08.896 --> 00:28:11.732
And how the pressure
to press your hair

00:28:11.732 --> 00:28:13.442
and straighten
your hair, and just

00:28:13.442 --> 00:28:17.654
my journey to have
natural hair as an adult.

00:28:17.654 --> 00:28:20.073
And so all those
things are sort of what

00:28:20.073 --> 00:28:21.450
it meant to shame
your hair, what

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:22.951
it meant to love your hair.

00:28:22.951 --> 00:28:26.079
How important it was to
see yourself on the screen.

00:28:26.079 --> 00:28:27.456
How important it
was for a father

00:28:27.456 --> 00:28:29.124
to kind of listen to
a daughter as she's

00:28:29.124 --> 00:28:32.336
sort of trying to sort of
go through her own hair

00:28:32.336 --> 00:28:33.545
love journey.

00:28:33.545 --> 00:28:37.716
So yeah, it really did
tap into all those things.

00:28:37.716 --> 00:28:40.928
And again, it just goes
to the very few images

00:28:40.928 --> 00:28:42.471
that are out there.

00:28:42.471 --> 00:28:45.015
We were so impressed with
how much people saw it,

00:28:45.015 --> 00:28:46.183
and how much they needed it.

00:28:46.183 --> 00:28:49.353
And it just really kind of
fuels me to just go crazy,

00:28:49.353 --> 00:28:51.980
to just do as many more images
as we can that kind of tap

00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:55.108
into those issues.

00:28:55.108 --> 00:28:58.070
Well, it resonated on
so many levels with me.

00:28:58.070 --> 00:29:03.158
And I told people
who haven't seen it,

00:29:03.158 --> 00:29:04.660
just sit down and watch it.

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:08.705
And love having conversations
with people about it.

00:29:08.705 --> 00:29:16.546
You know, I had not thought
about women composers

00:29:16.546 --> 00:29:21.927
until I met Laura
about two years ago.

00:29:21.927 --> 00:29:24.554
And I don't know if you
remember our conversation--

00:29:24.554 --> 00:29:25.764
I do.

00:29:25.764 --> 00:29:27.349
I cornered you.

00:29:27.349 --> 00:29:30.519
You were very clear.

00:29:30.519 --> 00:29:35.941
And you might have
spoken about the Alliance

00:29:35.941 --> 00:29:37.150
for Women Composers.

00:29:40.153 --> 00:29:45.367
I know that visibility was
really important for you.

00:29:45.367 --> 00:29:51.707
Has the Alliance been able to
enhance the visibility of women

00:29:51.707 --> 00:29:53.709
composers?

00:29:53.709 --> 00:29:56.169
I think, shockingly, yes.

00:29:56.169 --> 00:29:58.839
I mean, first of all, I
want to go back to Geena

00:29:58.839 --> 00:30:01.216
and what she did.

00:30:01.216 --> 00:30:03.927
When Geena started
her institute,

00:30:03.927 --> 00:30:06.680
we couldn't even
talk about sexism.

00:30:06.680 --> 00:30:07.931
Like nobody.

00:30:07.931 --> 00:30:11.768
If you were interviewed
in a trade magazine,

00:30:11.768 --> 00:30:14.396
someone would say, well, what's
it like being a woman composer?

00:30:14.396 --> 00:30:16.231
You literally
couldn't answer it,

00:30:16.231 --> 00:30:18.358
because you were afraid
you would never work again.

00:30:18.358 --> 00:30:21.903
So Geena really, really
has skin in this game

00:30:21.903 --> 00:30:23.405
that is so, so significant.

00:30:23.405 --> 00:30:27.576
We're all so unbelievably
grateful to her.

00:30:27.576 --> 00:30:32.289
For the Alliance, what happened
is composers were included--

00:30:32.289 --> 00:30:34.624
there are a couple of major
researchers in Hollywood,

00:30:34.624 --> 00:30:38.545
as I know you know, but Dr.
Martha Lauzen is one of them.

00:30:38.545 --> 00:30:42.924
And she included women
composers in her figures

00:30:42.924 --> 00:30:45.761
for the first time in 2014.

00:30:45.761 --> 00:30:50.307
So numbers don't whine,
and numbers don't lie.

00:30:50.307 --> 00:30:52.267
So when you have a number--

00:30:52.267 --> 00:30:57.314
and in our case, that year
it was 2% of the top 250 box

00:30:57.314 --> 00:30:59.316
office films were
scored by women.

00:30:59.316 --> 00:31:06.114
Now we're up to 6%,
which is great, you know?

00:31:06.114 --> 00:31:08.366
It's better.

00:31:08.366 --> 00:31:11.912
But when you have a number,
then you can go to people.

00:31:11.912 --> 00:31:13.747
And it's not like
you're complaining,

00:31:13.747 --> 00:31:15.749
but you have something
just to show.

00:31:15.749 --> 00:31:17.834
And it's a very,
very simple thing.

00:31:17.834 --> 00:31:19.836
And what we did--

00:31:19.836 --> 00:31:22.381
I have a good friend who
is a political consultant.

00:31:22.381 --> 00:31:23.965
And she said, you
need a directory.

00:31:23.965 --> 00:31:25.467
You just need a directory.

00:31:25.467 --> 00:31:27.469
And I thought, OK,
well that's easy to do.

00:31:27.469 --> 00:31:31.223
Because there were a lot of
us, but nobody knew about us.

00:31:31.223 --> 00:31:34.851
So if you were looking for
a woman to score your film,

00:31:34.851 --> 00:31:37.979
you would be told by agents
and producers and studios

00:31:37.979 --> 00:31:40.107
that there just
weren't any women.

00:31:40.107 --> 00:31:44.152
And this is true across the
board for below line talent.

00:31:44.152 --> 00:31:47.489
And that was totally
a fiction, as well.

00:31:47.489 --> 00:31:49.491
So when we put this
directory together--

00:31:49.491 --> 00:31:54.121
and we started with 30, and
now I think there are 700--

00:31:54.121 --> 00:31:58.375
that was radical in its own
way by saying, we exist.

00:31:58.375 --> 00:32:03.046
And when you're looking for
a woman composer, or a woman

00:32:03.046 --> 00:32:09.553
composer who's Latina, or who is
Black, or who lives in Chicago,

00:32:09.553 --> 00:32:12.264
we can help you
find that person.

00:32:12.264 --> 00:32:17.018
And that, I think, has proved
to be very significant.

00:32:17.018 --> 00:32:21.773
And I think as a result also
of an increasing awareness,

00:32:21.773 --> 00:32:23.608
I think MeToo.

00:32:23.608 --> 00:32:26.153
I think Harvey's
falling and all of that.

00:32:26.153 --> 00:32:28.530
Now there are
diversity programs.

00:32:28.530 --> 00:32:32.868
Now certain studios have
diversity requirements

00:32:32.868 --> 00:32:34.953
in terms of who they pitch.

00:32:34.953 --> 00:32:38.039
As you probably know,
the Academy, we just

00:32:38.039 --> 00:32:41.084
did new diversity requirements
for the Best Picture.

00:32:41.084 --> 00:32:44.754
So there are these things
emerging, which are really,

00:32:44.754 --> 00:32:47.174
really significant
and important,

00:32:47.174 --> 00:32:49.509
I think will have an impact.

00:32:49.509 --> 00:32:53.763
But we still have a tremendous
amount of work to do,

00:32:53.763 --> 00:32:55.724
and a long way to go.

00:32:55.724 --> 00:32:56.933
Yeah, we do.

00:32:56.933 --> 00:33:00.520
But speaking of the
directory, Alma,

00:33:00.520 --> 00:33:05.942
you saw a similar need in
starting Free The Work.

00:33:05.942 --> 00:33:06.902
Mm-hmm.

00:33:06.902 --> 00:33:11.990
Yeah, we actually made also a
database, together with Laura,

00:33:11.990 --> 00:33:15.452
for composers as part
of our organization.

00:33:15.452 --> 00:33:17.078
Yeah.

00:33:17.078 --> 00:33:21.208
So are you seeing results
from Free The Work?

00:33:21.208 --> 00:33:24.294
And can you tell us a
little bit more about it?

00:33:24.294 --> 00:33:25.295
Yeah.

00:33:25.295 --> 00:33:30.467
So Free The Work, the way it
started it was Free The Bid.

00:33:30.467 --> 00:33:34.221
And the way it started was
in 2016, I was at the time

00:33:34.221 --> 00:33:35.347
a commercial director.

00:33:35.347 --> 00:33:39.267
It was before I made Honey Boy,
my first feature that got me

00:33:39.267 --> 00:33:40.977
the honor to be in the Academy.

00:33:40.977 --> 00:33:46.233
But really what was happening
is there was a three bid

00:33:46.233 --> 00:33:50.654
system where on every commercial
and every advertisement that

00:33:50.654 --> 00:33:53.698
was done, advertising
agencies and brands would have

00:33:53.698 --> 00:33:55.825
three directors bid on them.

00:33:55.825 --> 00:33:57.202
And it was always three men.

00:33:57.202 --> 00:34:01.623
So I was starting to get
really big campaigns,

00:34:01.623 --> 00:34:04.709
and noticed that I'm always
bidding against two men.

00:34:04.709 --> 00:34:07.879
And there's never any
women around me almost.

00:34:07.879 --> 00:34:09.965
Of course, there
were a few, but we

00:34:09.965 --> 00:34:14.094
were kind of traveling
in different circles.

00:34:14.094 --> 00:34:16.846
Almost every production company
only represented one or two

00:34:16.846 --> 00:34:20.809
women in their rosters, and
they would have 10, 20 men.

00:34:20.809 --> 00:34:22.435
So I started Free
The Bid, which was

00:34:22.435 --> 00:34:27.232
kind of a way to say we're
not demanding you do hire us,

00:34:27.232 --> 00:34:29.359
but we're demanding
that you let us bid.

00:34:29.359 --> 00:34:32.946
Because the excuse was that
our reels are not big enough.

00:34:32.946 --> 00:34:37.117
And it was like, this cycle of
endless lack of opportunity.

00:34:37.117 --> 00:34:38.660
Because you can't
make you reel bigger

00:34:38.660 --> 00:34:39.953
if you're never even bidding.

00:34:39.953 --> 00:34:44.207
So the idea was, OK, we're going
to get all these brands to say,

00:34:44.207 --> 00:34:47.711
one of the three
has to be a woman.

00:34:47.711 --> 00:34:50.755
And within one year--
and we built a database.

00:34:50.755 --> 00:34:53.383
I built a database back
then in my kitchen,

00:34:53.383 --> 00:34:56.845
which was on my laptop, which
was just all these directors.

00:34:56.845 --> 00:34:59.973
It was about I think 100
directors at the time

00:34:59.973 --> 00:35:01.391
when we started.

00:35:01.391 --> 00:35:04.019
And within the first
year, we took brands

00:35:04.019 --> 00:35:06.730
like HP, who had
zero women directors

00:35:06.730 --> 00:35:11.860
on all of their global
campaigns, to 58% women

00:35:11.860 --> 00:35:13.403
directors.

00:35:13.403 --> 00:35:15.739
And we worked with them
to kind of make sure

00:35:15.739 --> 00:35:18.825
that once women were in
the room and got to bid

00:35:18.825 --> 00:35:22.662
and got to pitch, a lot of
them obviously got the job.

00:35:22.662 --> 00:35:25.957
Because they had great ideas
and they were very competitive,

00:35:25.957 --> 00:35:30.128
and they did the work better
than anybody in some cases.

00:35:30.128 --> 00:35:33.632
So it made me see the
lack of opportunity

00:35:33.632 --> 00:35:37.135
not just for women, but
also for immigrants,

00:35:37.135 --> 00:35:38.970
for people of color,
for trans women, which

00:35:38.970 --> 00:35:43.725
should be included in this
conversation, and other gender

00:35:43.725 --> 00:35:45.101
expressions and preferences.

00:35:45.101 --> 00:35:47.896
So we started Free
The Work, and tried

00:35:47.896 --> 00:35:52.317
to really bring the same
elements into film and TV.

00:35:52.317 --> 00:35:54.736
And our database
now today, we have

00:35:54.736 --> 00:35:57.238
partners like Amazon
Studios, Jen Salke,

00:35:57.238 --> 00:35:59.449
who's been a really
great partner to us.

00:35:59.449 --> 00:36:02.911
And Procter &amp; Gamble,
which almost reached parity

00:36:02.911 --> 00:36:04.037
with most of their brands.

00:36:04.037 --> 00:36:06.039
And they are the biggest
advertiser in the world.

00:36:06.039 --> 00:36:09.626
So we're both in
film and TV, and we

00:36:09.626 --> 00:36:15.256
have over 2,000 creators around
the world in 45 countries which

00:36:15.256 --> 00:36:16.091
we operate in.

00:36:16.091 --> 00:36:18.134
And we also do programs
in those countries,

00:36:18.134 --> 00:36:20.053
and we have ambassadors
in each one of them.

00:36:20.053 --> 00:36:23.932
And kind of study the
inequality in those places.

00:36:23.932 --> 00:36:26.518
But really what it is,
is it's a tool that

00:36:26.518 --> 00:36:31.439
allows you to bring tech in the
same way that Spotify, I guess,

00:36:31.439 --> 00:36:35.652
is our model in some ways
brought tech into the ability

00:36:35.652 --> 00:36:37.946
to search for music.

00:36:37.946 --> 00:36:40.407
You could search for directors,
for cinematographers,

00:36:40.407 --> 00:36:43.284
for editors, for composers.

00:36:43.284 --> 00:36:45.787
And for other below
the line roles

00:36:45.787 --> 00:36:48.581
that we're slowly building,
like colorists and editors.

00:36:48.581 --> 00:36:51.960
And it kind of
recommends you, also,

00:36:51.960 --> 00:36:53.670
artists based on your search.

00:36:53.670 --> 00:36:56.172
And you can make
playlists, short lists,

00:36:56.172 --> 00:36:57.632
and share them with
your producers.

00:36:57.632 --> 00:37:01.469
So it's really a tool for
people to find talent.

00:37:01.469 --> 00:37:03.888
When you ask if we make
change, I know we make change.

00:37:03.888 --> 00:37:08.393
But I also know that
at the end of the day,

00:37:08.393 --> 00:37:14.274
I still get those texts at
11:00 at night from a producer

00:37:14.274 --> 00:37:17.610
saying, hey, we really
were told by such and such

00:37:17.610 --> 00:37:20.780
that they want us
to hire a woman.

00:37:20.780 --> 00:37:22.991
And can you help us find it?

00:37:22.991 --> 00:37:29.581
So there's still this
component of whenever

00:37:29.581 --> 00:37:34.586
the clock is ticking and
people have less and less time,

00:37:34.586 --> 00:37:38.423
somehow they don't write
it into their schedule

00:37:38.423 --> 00:37:40.341
to discover talent.

00:37:40.341 --> 00:37:44.304
And to discover the women
or the people of color--

00:37:44.304 --> 00:37:45.763
Black identified or other.

00:37:48.391 --> 00:37:51.352
They're searching for something,
and in the last minute

00:37:51.352 --> 00:37:55.315
they find themselves saying,
oh my God, I don't know any.

00:37:55.315 --> 00:37:58.610
So it's not-- so I can still
see that it's not a long year

00:37:58.610 --> 00:38:01.654
organic process of
people educating

00:38:01.654 --> 00:38:03.448
themselves, and
making sure that they

00:38:03.448 --> 00:38:06.493
have their favorite directors
in all of those categories.

00:38:06.493 --> 00:38:08.661
If I would corner them
and ask them who's

00:38:08.661 --> 00:38:11.456
their favorite 10 whatever--

00:38:11.456 --> 00:38:12.499
probably all-male.

00:38:12.499 --> 00:38:13.958
And then at the
last minute they'll

00:38:13.958 --> 00:38:15.794
text me or look at my
site to find somebody

00:38:15.794 --> 00:38:17.504
so they can recommend it.

00:38:17.504 --> 00:38:19.672
I'm waiting for
the moment where I

00:38:19.672 --> 00:38:22.801
want to see the excitement
about discovering talent.

00:38:22.801 --> 00:38:27.722
I want to see the excitement
of people about hiring women.

00:38:27.722 --> 00:38:31.643
So it's not just a
chore, or something

00:38:31.643 --> 00:38:34.062
that you're being pushed
to do because of some--

00:38:34.062 --> 00:38:36.439
I think a lot of people
have this idea of,

00:38:36.439 --> 00:38:40.693
you know, we can check
the box with women.

00:38:40.693 --> 00:38:45.156
As opposed to really be
being engaged and committed

00:38:45.156 --> 00:38:49.077
to having better
gender representation.

00:38:49.077 --> 00:38:52.956
It's just something that you
have to sort of do, and then

00:38:52.956 --> 00:38:56.376
move on to the things that
are more important to you.

00:38:56.376 --> 00:38:58.837
There's so much we
haven't covered,

00:38:58.837 --> 00:39:02.882
but I really want us to think
about some of the challenges

00:39:02.882 --> 00:39:03.758
that we still face.

00:39:03.758 --> 00:39:07.178
And one of the things that I
am absolutely certain that we

00:39:07.178 --> 00:39:10.265
are all still
facing is a subject

00:39:10.265 --> 00:39:12.684
that has been brought
up in different ways,

00:39:12.684 --> 00:39:17.313
but that is how do we make
sure that when we say gender

00:39:17.313 --> 00:39:21.317
that we're covering the
broad range of gender,

00:39:21.317 --> 00:39:25.446
in terms of all
kinds of diversity

00:39:25.446 --> 00:39:28.157
that seems to be missing.

00:39:28.157 --> 00:39:33.997
And until we have it, we
cannot have full gender equity.

00:39:33.997 --> 00:39:40.169
So I want to ask
you, how do we really

00:39:40.169 --> 00:39:44.048
begin to have that
full understanding

00:39:44.048 --> 00:39:45.383
of what gender means?

00:39:45.383 --> 00:39:53.016
How do we-- how does gender
inclusiveness and diversity

00:39:53.016 --> 00:39:57.145
figure into your work?

00:39:57.145 --> 00:39:59.480
And I'll just start
off by saying,

00:39:59.480 --> 00:40:04.235
I heard you mention it,
Laura, in your creative work,

00:40:04.235 --> 00:40:09.949
about composing music
for Black women.

00:40:09.949 --> 00:40:14.495
And I know, Geena, you have
initiatives that involve

00:40:14.495 --> 00:40:17.665
inclusion and diversity.

00:40:17.665 --> 00:40:21.878
Can you just tell me more,
each of you tell me more,

00:40:21.878 --> 00:40:25.006
about those?

00:40:25.006 --> 00:40:25.506
Yeah.

00:40:25.506 --> 00:40:32.263
Well, so we have always
used an intersectional lens

00:40:32.263 --> 00:40:35.892
when we're talking about this.

00:40:35.892 --> 00:40:37.727
You know, it's called--

00:40:37.727 --> 00:40:42.565
my institute is on
gender in media,

00:40:42.565 --> 00:40:47.737
but really we're looking at
all underrepresented segments

00:40:47.737 --> 00:40:48.655
of the population.

00:40:51.449 --> 00:40:55.953
Because you know, the goal
is let's start reflecting

00:40:55.953 --> 00:40:57.372
the population as it is--

00:40:59.916 --> 00:41:02.126
because we judge our
value by seeing ourselves

00:41:02.126 --> 00:41:04.128
reflected in the
popular culture.

00:41:04.128 --> 00:41:05.797
Oh, there's somebody
that looks like me.

00:41:05.797 --> 00:41:08.883
I must be valuable
to this society.

00:41:08.883 --> 00:41:11.969
And if you don't see
yourself, you get the message

00:41:11.969 --> 00:41:15.264
that you're not worth anything.

00:41:15.264 --> 00:41:23.189
And so as you said, I think
it is very, very important

00:41:23.189 --> 00:41:26.943
to cast as broad a
net as you possibly

00:41:26.943 --> 00:41:29.737
can to define inclusion.

00:41:29.737 --> 00:41:33.700
And as you said just
a little bit ago,

00:41:33.700 --> 00:41:34.909
it's not just checking a box.

00:41:34.909 --> 00:41:35.993
Well, we have a woman now.

00:41:35.993 --> 00:41:38.621
So we already did that.

00:41:38.621 --> 00:41:41.040
We are fully half
of the population,

00:41:41.040 --> 00:41:44.419
and there's all versions
of being a woman

00:41:44.419 --> 00:41:47.505
that we need to embrace.

00:41:47.505 --> 00:41:53.845
So Karen, one of the things
that you have done recently

00:41:53.845 --> 00:41:56.097
is that you have
written a letter that

00:41:56.097 --> 00:42:02.270
has become sort of an open
letter to women in animation.

00:42:02.270 --> 00:42:07.358
And I will let you tell
us about that letter.

00:42:07.358 --> 00:42:09.777
Because the question
that I want to ask,

00:42:09.777 --> 00:42:12.905
though, is when you
think of the approach

00:42:12.905 --> 00:42:15.199
that you took
women in animation,

00:42:15.199 --> 00:42:21.289
is developing allies really
critical when it comes

00:42:21.289 --> 00:42:25.334
to creating these inclusive
communities that we all

00:42:25.334 --> 00:42:27.503
want to live and work in?

00:42:27.503 --> 00:42:28.004
Yes.

00:42:28.004 --> 00:42:29.589
I think that that
is the thing that--

00:42:29.589 --> 00:42:31.507
things that keep me up
at night are the things

00:42:31.507 --> 00:42:34.177
that Alma and Geena
are mentioning.

00:42:34.177 --> 00:42:35.928
That we want those--

00:42:35.928 --> 00:42:39.682
we want our work to reflect
the world that we live in,

00:42:39.682 --> 00:42:42.435
and we know that the people
that are marginalized

00:42:42.435 --> 00:42:44.395
understand why they
want a seat at the table

00:42:44.395 --> 00:42:46.189
and they want their voice heard.

00:42:46.189 --> 00:42:49.150
But until the people that
have the seat at the table

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:51.903
and already have their
voice heard understand it

00:42:51.903 --> 00:42:55.656
from a very just gut level
that that has to happen

00:42:55.656 --> 00:42:57.492
and they want it,
then we're going

00:42:57.492 --> 00:43:00.453
to be putting
requirements and quotas.

00:43:00.453 --> 00:43:03.831
Or if there's not women that are
leading and making decisions,

00:43:03.831 --> 00:43:05.958
if we're off on a
vacation, what decisions

00:43:05.958 --> 00:43:07.210
would be made when we're gone?

00:43:07.210 --> 00:43:11.047
It's like how does it become
so systemic in that way

00:43:11.047 --> 00:43:12.256
that it just is?

00:43:12.256 --> 00:43:14.926
It just is what it is.

00:43:14.926 --> 00:43:17.011
I remember, Geena,
when the Institute--

00:43:17.011 --> 00:43:19.806
when I was first aware of it,
it seemed so revolutionary.

00:43:19.806 --> 00:43:21.849
But it's like, I can't
wait until the future where

00:43:21.849 --> 00:43:22.600
it's like, of course.

00:43:22.600 --> 00:43:23.768
It's like you don't need it.

00:43:23.768 --> 00:43:25.394
You know, it just is what it is.

00:43:25.394 --> 00:43:29.732
But the letter you're referring
to was after George Floyd.

00:43:29.732 --> 00:43:31.859
Marge Dean, who runs
Women in Animation,

00:43:31.859 --> 00:43:36.739
wanted women of color to kind of
speak to how they were feeling.

00:43:36.739 --> 00:43:39.492
And you know, how I was
feeling was in Los Angeles,

00:43:39.492 --> 00:43:42.286
there were riots and
there were things

00:43:42.286 --> 00:43:43.830
that were happening
at The Grove,

00:43:43.830 --> 00:43:45.790
and places very close
to us and dear to us.

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:49.001
And sort of the next morning,
one of the newscasters

00:43:49.001 --> 00:43:51.462
said, but LA will bounce back.

00:43:51.462 --> 00:43:52.839
We always will.

00:43:52.839 --> 00:43:55.007
And that's what kind of
just struck my nerve.

00:43:55.007 --> 00:43:57.134
Well, part of LA will
bounce back, but what

00:43:57.134 --> 00:43:59.512
about the underlying problem?

00:43:59.512 --> 00:44:02.598
And you know, again, I've
got two teenage boys that

00:44:02.598 --> 00:44:05.601
are roaming the earth now and
doing things that they want

00:44:05.601 --> 00:44:06.853
to do, and being silly kids.

00:44:06.853 --> 00:44:09.981
And I'm scared every time
they go out of the house.

00:44:09.981 --> 00:44:11.816
And you know, what
I was just imploring

00:44:11.816 --> 00:44:16.154
is that in every moment
in civil rights movements,

00:44:16.154 --> 00:44:19.532
for people of color at least,
it's the non-marginalized,

00:44:19.532 --> 00:44:20.950
it's the white
people, that needed

00:44:20.950 --> 00:44:26.789
to be allies for the cause when
it really kind of took hold.

00:44:26.789 --> 00:44:29.500
And so that really was my
plea to women in animation.

00:44:29.500 --> 00:44:31.669
Like we have to
make these changes.

00:44:31.669 --> 00:44:34.255
We have to make them
quick and in a hurry.

00:44:34.255 --> 00:44:38.301
We have to not be
polite anymore.

00:44:38.301 --> 00:44:40.887
We have to sort of be pretty
aggressive about the way

00:44:40.887 --> 00:44:42.263
that we do it.

00:44:42.263 --> 00:44:44.640
And we need those allies,
and we absolutely need it

00:44:44.640 --> 00:44:45.766
in the gender discussion.

00:44:45.766 --> 00:44:49.478
And I agree that this is
really bigger than-- you know,

00:44:49.478 --> 00:44:53.524
I'm on a counsel at Sony to kind
of respond to the George Floyd

00:44:53.524 --> 00:44:54.692
social justice.

00:44:54.692 --> 00:44:56.652
But really, we're
talking about sort

00:44:56.652 --> 00:44:58.738
of Black numbers in our movies.

00:44:58.738 --> 00:45:02.909
But really once we tackle
any marginalized community,

00:45:02.909 --> 00:45:04.785
we do the same for--

00:45:04.785 --> 00:45:09.707
it's the same struggle, but
we need the majority to really

00:45:09.707 --> 00:45:11.292
kind of see it in
the same way that we

00:45:11.292 --> 00:45:13.920
know it's as urgent as it is.

00:45:13.920 --> 00:45:15.546
Right.

00:45:15.546 --> 00:45:21.093
I know, Laura, that when you
became a member of the Academy,

00:45:21.093 --> 00:45:26.724
that you were the woman
group in the music group.

00:45:26.724 --> 00:45:29.727
How much has changed?

00:45:29.727 --> 00:45:32.188
Do you see progress
in the Academy?

00:45:32.188 --> 00:45:35.816
I know they're hosting us, but
we can talk frankly about them,

00:45:35.816 --> 00:45:40.571
because these dialogues are
about frank conversations.

00:45:40.571 --> 00:45:43.699
How much progress have you seen?

00:45:43.699 --> 00:45:47.703
And how much do you
think it's still

00:45:47.703 --> 00:45:53.292
a case that women are the
only women in the room when

00:45:53.292 --> 00:45:55.211
important decisions
are being made,

00:45:55.211 --> 00:45:58.631
or discussions are being had?

00:45:58.631 --> 00:46:01.509
I think all of it's
true to an extent.

00:46:01.509 --> 00:46:04.804
I was admitted to
the Academy in 2015,

00:46:04.804 --> 00:46:09.517
and I basically got sponsored
to be a member of the Academy.

00:46:09.517 --> 00:46:11.310
I never thought I would
get in, and then I

00:46:11.310 --> 00:46:12.937
was going to make a
big stink about it.

00:46:12.937 --> 00:46:15.731
That was my big plan.

00:46:15.731 --> 00:46:17.775
And then I got in.

00:46:17.775 --> 00:46:20.861
But it was something
that I never

00:46:20.861 --> 00:46:23.531
felt that I would be included
in, because it was always

00:46:23.531 --> 00:46:25.825
very guarded.

00:46:25.825 --> 00:46:27.368
and it was an insane idea

00:46:27.368 --> 00:46:28.995
to run for governor
after I'd been

00:46:28.995 --> 00:46:30.830
a member only for a few months.

00:46:30.830 --> 00:46:33.624
But I got into the room.

00:46:33.624 --> 00:46:35.167
And what happened
is the first time I

00:46:35.167 --> 00:46:37.503
was in on an executive
committee meeting, which

00:46:37.503 --> 00:46:41.132
was the kind of smaller groups
associated with each branch,

00:46:41.132 --> 00:46:42.550
was the membership meeting.

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:46.137
And I was literally allowed
a seat at the table.

00:46:46.137 --> 00:46:48.097
At that point, there
was one other woman

00:46:48.097 --> 00:46:52.018
in the world, Siedah Garrett--
great songwriter and lyricist.

00:46:52.018 --> 00:46:55.896
She had been the only woman
in the room for 10 years.

00:46:55.896 --> 00:47:00.151
So it was me and Siedah,
and they were trying

00:47:00.151 --> 00:47:01.986
to make a big diversity push.

00:47:01.986 --> 00:47:05.865
And I had brought all
these names to the table.

00:47:05.865 --> 00:47:09.201
And the first one
began with a B.

00:47:09.201 --> 00:47:11.287
So you go down the
list alphabetically.

00:47:11.287 --> 00:47:14.457
So right off the bat,
my first academy meeting

00:47:14.457 --> 00:47:18.919
I had to strongly advocate
for a woman composer.

00:47:18.919 --> 00:47:22.465
And what I had to do with
that time was explain--

00:47:22.465 --> 00:47:24.550
because her credits
were being dismissed

00:47:24.550 --> 00:47:26.635
as not being Academy worthy.

00:47:26.635 --> 00:47:30.890
And what I had to explain was
her credits in the playing

00:47:30.890 --> 00:47:34.018
field that we are
given were massive.

00:47:34.018 --> 00:47:36.395
And I pointed to
the guys and I said,

00:47:36.395 --> 00:47:40.858
this is like you scoring a Pixar
film, the fact that she scored

00:47:40.858 --> 00:47:42.693
this film and it won Sundance.

00:47:42.693 --> 00:47:44.779
It's that big a deal.

00:47:44.779 --> 00:47:48.366
And so I was able to kind of--

00:47:48.366 --> 00:47:49.742
and it was painful--

00:47:49.742 --> 00:47:53.162
but I was able to
educate a roomful of men

00:47:53.162 --> 00:47:57.208
about the differences between
what we were at that point

00:47:57.208 --> 00:48:00.711
allowed to do and the
opportunities that they had.

00:48:00.711 --> 00:48:02.922
And I think honestly as
a result of that meeting,

00:48:02.922 --> 00:48:04.215
I was elected governor.

00:48:04.215 --> 00:48:06.634
Because I think that
my branch realized

00:48:06.634 --> 00:48:09.512
that there was a real
need for this, and because

00:48:09.512 --> 00:48:11.847
of the advocacy work that I'd
done through the Alliance.

00:48:11.847 --> 00:48:14.433
But I have to say
that at that point,

00:48:14.433 --> 00:48:18.020
I'd been an advocate
sort of very publicly

00:48:18.020 --> 00:48:19.563
for about three or four years.

00:48:19.563 --> 00:48:22.274
And in that moment in that three
hours of the Academy meeting,

00:48:22.274 --> 00:48:25.903
I felt that I had done
more good than I'd

00:48:25.903 --> 00:48:30.574
done in the entire three years
before, because of the level

00:48:30.574 --> 00:48:31.742
that I was playing at.

00:48:31.742 --> 00:48:36.497
So I, with a lot of
really wonderful men

00:48:36.497 --> 00:48:39.500
and now women-- because all
those women that were admitted

00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:42.878
are now sitting on that
executive committee, right?

00:48:42.878 --> 00:48:44.880
So that's how it really happens.

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:47.299
You know, that
you get people in,

00:48:47.299 --> 00:48:51.887
and then you get them a seat
at the table, literally.

00:48:51.887 --> 00:48:53.347
And then changes happen.

00:48:53.347 --> 00:48:56.684
And you find these ways that
you can expand the rules.

00:48:56.684 --> 00:48:59.311
For example, we
started a short list,

00:48:59.311 --> 00:49:01.564
because I and
other people really

00:49:01.564 --> 00:49:03.732
believed that by
having a short list,

00:49:03.732 --> 00:49:07.820
you could see more diversity in
that short list instead of just

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:09.738
the five nominees.

00:49:09.738 --> 00:49:13.325
So last year we had Michael
Abels, we had Kathryn Bostic.

00:49:13.325 --> 00:49:18.664
We had people who might not
have been amplified if only

00:49:18.664 --> 00:49:20.249
the nominees were amplified.

00:49:20.249 --> 00:49:23.586
So I think it's been, for me,
working in an institution has

00:49:23.586 --> 00:49:24.587
been really interesting.

00:49:24.587 --> 00:49:27.965
Because you figure out
how to navigate the rules,

00:49:27.965 --> 00:49:30.676
change the ones that you
need to change, and then

00:49:30.676 --> 00:49:34.889
make them work for you in order
to push the field forward.

00:49:34.889 --> 00:49:38.309
And I do think that an
Academy membership has

00:49:38.309 --> 00:49:39.685
been really helpful to people.

00:49:39.685 --> 00:49:42.062
It elevates people.

00:49:42.062 --> 00:49:44.106
And so it's been good work.

00:49:44.106 --> 00:49:46.317
The board room is
way more diverse

00:49:46.317 --> 00:49:48.986
than it was when I
got there, in terms

00:49:48.986 --> 00:49:52.031
of people of color and women.

00:49:52.031 --> 00:49:53.699
It's much, much better.

00:49:53.699 --> 00:49:55.201
And we've had some
big victories.

00:49:55.201 --> 00:49:58.245
And now it's time
to turn the page

00:49:58.245 --> 00:50:01.624
and to move to the next stage.

00:50:01.624 --> 00:50:03.834
And what I've always
said is that I

00:50:03.834 --> 00:50:06.212
want the Alliance to grow
bigger than you can possibly

00:50:06.212 --> 00:50:09.298
imagine in five years, and
then we want to be gone.

00:50:09.298 --> 00:50:11.842
Because at some
point, all of us want

00:50:11.842 --> 00:50:13.928
to stop talking
about this, right?

00:50:13.928 --> 00:50:18.849
All of us want to go
beyond continuing to need

00:50:18.849 --> 00:50:20.851
to have this conversation.

00:50:20.851 --> 00:50:24.271
So the goal is to have a
completely equal playing field,

00:50:24.271 --> 00:50:28.400
where you're known for,
oh, she did this score.

00:50:28.400 --> 00:50:30.402
Or oh, she made that
movie and I love it,

00:50:30.402 --> 00:50:32.863
so let's hire her
to do this or that.

00:50:32.863 --> 00:50:35.115
That's where we all want to get.

00:50:35.115 --> 00:50:37.534
And it's wonderful to
have these conversations,

00:50:37.534 --> 00:50:40.871
and I can't wait until
we stop having them, too.

00:50:40.871 --> 00:50:42.665
It's almost as
though you're saying,

00:50:42.665 --> 00:50:45.501
I want to work my
way out of the job.

00:50:45.501 --> 00:50:46.085
Yeah.

00:50:46.085 --> 00:50:46.585
Yeah.

00:50:46.585 --> 00:50:50.047
Out of the-- certainly I want
to keep composing until nobody

00:50:50.047 --> 00:50:51.548
wants to hear from me anymore.

00:50:51.548 --> 00:50:55.010
But I think we all want
to work our way out

00:50:55.010 --> 00:50:59.807
of the advocacy
jobs, and go back

00:50:59.807 --> 00:51:01.225
to being full time artists.

00:51:01.225 --> 00:51:03.936
But in fact, that's
impossible to do

00:51:03.936 --> 00:51:06.355
until we've opened
this thing up,

00:51:06.355 --> 00:51:11.694
and we've cleared the
pipeline for younger women

00:51:11.694 --> 00:51:13.737
and underrepresented people.

00:51:13.737 --> 00:51:17.074
And we see people at
the top doing whatever

00:51:17.074 --> 00:51:20.119
work they want to do
without being typecast

00:51:20.119 --> 00:51:24.123
or having their qualifications
constantly questioned.

00:51:24.123 --> 00:51:27.626
I'm going to end
with two questions--

00:51:27.626 --> 00:51:29.586
one word questions.

00:51:29.586 --> 00:51:33.591
And they are this--

00:51:33.591 --> 00:51:38.679
first of all, in one
word, where do you

00:51:38.679 --> 00:51:42.808
want to see more gender balance?

00:51:42.808 --> 00:51:46.186
Secondly, what gives you hope?

00:51:48.772 --> 00:51:50.858
So those two questions,
you can think about those.

00:51:50.858 --> 00:51:53.193
And you had a comment, Alma.

00:51:53.193 --> 00:51:57.614
Actually, my comment is actually
an answer to your question.

00:51:57.614 --> 00:52:01.952
So I should have just
been patient and waited.

00:52:01.952 --> 00:52:05.247
But I am an Israeli,
so we always

00:52:05.247 --> 00:52:10.836
jump ahead and lack those
talents of being patient.

00:52:10.836 --> 00:52:14.089
So yeah, my answer is actually
exactly what you were saying.

00:52:14.089 --> 00:52:18.510
Because Laura said something
so important to me, which

00:52:18.510 --> 00:52:21.805
is she said, we can't wait
to go back to being an artist

00:52:21.805 --> 00:52:25.267
full time and not need to
necessarily do the advocacy.

00:52:25.267 --> 00:52:28.395
And I think a lot of artists
and a lot of filmmakers

00:52:28.395 --> 00:52:35.527
that do the work feel the
toll of needing to activate

00:52:35.527 --> 00:52:39.573
the parts of their brain
and their social skills

00:52:39.573 --> 00:52:42.993
and their advocacy and
activism that sometimes often

00:52:42.993 --> 00:52:45.329
really bite into their
time of being an artist.

00:52:45.329 --> 00:52:50.834
And they start to question
themselves, who am I right now?

00:52:50.834 --> 00:52:54.546
And the thing that really has
touched me this year, when

00:52:54.546 --> 00:52:56.840
we speak about institutions
and organizations--

00:52:56.840 --> 00:52:59.510
we just spoke about
the Academy, but I

00:52:59.510 --> 00:53:03.013
had the honor to win
a DGA award this year

00:53:03.013 --> 00:53:05.182
as a director for Honey Boy.

00:53:05.182 --> 00:53:07.851
And when I was there,
I really kind of

00:53:07.851 --> 00:53:11.063
took the time, the few
minutes I had on stage,

00:53:11.063 --> 00:53:14.691
to speak about the Parenthood
Penalty campaign, which

00:53:14.691 --> 00:53:17.361
I was part of and
I know Geena, I

00:53:17.361 --> 00:53:22.116
believe you also were
part of that campaign.

00:53:22.116 --> 00:53:25.536
And you asked where else we want
to see this gender equality,

00:53:25.536 --> 00:53:29.248
and really to me it's
kind of like it's not just

00:53:29.248 --> 00:53:30.290
about the representation.

00:53:30.290 --> 00:53:32.960
It's not just about
finding the talent.

00:53:32.960 --> 00:53:35.546
It's about really understanding
that the whole system is

00:53:35.546 --> 00:53:36.088
rigged.

00:53:36.088 --> 00:53:39.591
That everything was
built by men for men.

00:53:39.591 --> 00:53:42.678
And for all of us to
really thrive and allow

00:53:42.678 --> 00:53:45.347
for these talents,
past their first film

00:53:45.347 --> 00:53:50.102
or past their experiences that
kind of get them discovered

00:53:50.102 --> 00:53:53.981
or seen, how do they
survive past that when

00:53:53.981 --> 00:53:57.568
women lose their health
insurance in their most

00:53:57.568 --> 00:53:58.902
vulnerable year?

00:53:58.902 --> 00:54:02.322
Because the way the
insurance was built

00:54:02.322 --> 00:54:06.326
is that if you do not show up
enough in that year on sets,

00:54:06.326 --> 00:54:09.204
you lose it if you
don't have enough days.

00:54:09.204 --> 00:54:12.249
And the fact that
parenthood in general

00:54:12.249 --> 00:54:15.169
is being looked at as
something that just women do,

00:54:15.169 --> 00:54:18.172
as opposed to men, too.

00:54:18.172 --> 00:54:22.301
So I think what we
have to look at is--

00:54:22.301 --> 00:54:24.678
and where I would love to
see more gender equality--

00:54:24.678 --> 00:54:28.557
is not just in
discovering the talent,

00:54:28.557 --> 00:54:32.978
but making sure that that
talent has a sustainable career.

00:54:32.978 --> 00:54:38.150
That the things that
are supposed to support,

00:54:38.150 --> 00:54:41.904
that are already supporting
men, would support also

00:54:41.904 --> 00:54:45.199
underrepresented creators,
whether it's people of color,

00:54:45.199 --> 00:54:49.369
or of certain social economical,
or immigrants, or women,

00:54:49.369 --> 00:54:50.120
trans women.

00:54:50.120 --> 00:54:53.916
And all these people that really
don't have a chance sometimes,

00:54:53.916 --> 00:54:56.210
even once they get
discovered, because

00:54:56.210 --> 00:55:00.339
of all sorts of things that
we're not taking into account.

00:55:00.339 --> 00:55:03.300
So more-- I'm going
to chalk that up

00:55:03.300 --> 00:55:06.803
as more balance toward
parenting responsibilities.

00:55:06.803 --> 00:55:09.515
I'm looking at--

00:55:09.515 --> 00:55:12.976
a system is made up of parts.

00:55:12.976 --> 00:55:18.148
So what are the parts
that you all see?

00:55:18.148 --> 00:55:21.360
And you've mentioned
parenting and being

00:55:21.360 --> 00:55:24.780
a penalty for parenting.

00:55:24.780 --> 00:55:28.033
So that's just one
part of the system.

00:55:28.033 --> 00:55:30.869
There are many parts, and I
think we think of the system.

00:55:30.869 --> 00:55:32.746
But it's hard for
people at times

00:55:32.746 --> 00:55:34.915
to really comprehend
the entire system.

00:55:34.915 --> 00:55:38.377
And so sometimes it's
important for people to think,

00:55:38.377 --> 00:55:44.800
what is the one part that I
know needs to be more fair, more

00:55:44.800 --> 00:55:48.845
inclusive, better
resourced, in order

00:55:48.845 --> 00:55:52.558
to improve gender balance?

00:55:52.558 --> 00:55:57.938
Any other examples that
any of you could give?

00:55:57.938 --> 00:56:01.191
I have a kind of a little bit
of a different answer in that I

00:56:01.191 --> 00:56:02.484
want--

00:56:02.484 --> 00:56:04.069
when you're talking
about the Academy,

00:56:04.069 --> 00:56:06.780
I want voters to appreciate
the female aperture.

00:56:06.780 --> 00:56:09.783
And I think that sometimes it's
difficult for people to see.

00:56:09.783 --> 00:56:12.911
And the thing that struck
me is at the end of Little

00:56:12.911 --> 00:56:17.583
Women, which really
had incredible moments

00:56:17.583 --> 00:56:21.086
from the female aperture, she
gives the baby to the husband

00:56:21.086 --> 00:56:21.795
as she's walking.

00:56:21.795 --> 00:56:24.006
Do you guys remember that scene?

00:56:24.006 --> 00:56:27.968
This is-- I can't remember
the year of Little Women,

00:56:27.968 --> 00:56:31.263
but let's say it's 1870 or 1872.

00:56:31.263 --> 00:56:34.641
She gives the baby to the
husband, who's got the thing--

00:56:34.641 --> 00:56:36.351
speaking of parenting--
on his chest,

00:56:36.351 --> 00:56:38.270
you know, that they
put the baby into.

00:56:38.270 --> 00:56:42.274
That was such a moment of
female aperture and celebration.

00:56:42.274 --> 00:56:45.861
Gives the baby to the husband,
and then joins her sisters.

00:56:45.861 --> 00:56:50.365
And I think that people
are not understanding

00:56:50.365 --> 00:56:56.371
what women and all kinds
of underrepresented groups

00:56:56.371 --> 00:56:58.498
are bringing to the table.

00:56:58.498 --> 00:57:01.710
And sometimes it can
be super obvious,

00:57:01.710 --> 00:57:04.004
and sometimes it
can be super subtle.

00:57:04.004 --> 00:57:05.547
And I think that
this is when you

00:57:05.547 --> 00:57:09.092
look at the diversity
of experience,

00:57:09.092 --> 00:57:12.679
and sort of the diversity
of the human condition.

00:57:12.679 --> 00:57:18.852
This is how it really can affect
our field and what we're doing.

00:57:18.852 --> 00:57:20.812
These apertures are
really significant,

00:57:20.812 --> 00:57:22.397
and I appreciate
them when I see them.

00:57:22.397 --> 00:57:25.150
And I think other
people do, too.

00:57:25.150 --> 00:57:26.526
In terms of the hope--

00:57:26.526 --> 00:57:27.444
my students.

00:57:27.444 --> 00:57:29.404
I mean, I teach at USC.

00:57:29.404 --> 00:57:31.365
The women are so aware.

00:57:31.365 --> 00:57:33.158
They support each other.

00:57:33.158 --> 00:57:34.743
They feel supported.

00:57:34.743 --> 00:57:36.662
They know they
have a place to go

00:57:36.662 --> 00:57:40.499
in terms of in my particular
field, the Alliance, or Women

00:57:40.499 --> 00:57:43.627
in Film and other organizations
that have been started.

00:57:43.627 --> 00:57:46.463
And they're aware,
and they know what

00:57:46.463 --> 00:57:47.923
they're getting
into, which I think

00:57:47.923 --> 00:57:49.591
is a really good
thing at this point.

00:57:49.591 --> 00:57:50.801
Right.

00:57:50.801 --> 00:57:56.139
I would say that my theory is
that if it happens on screen,

00:57:56.139 --> 00:57:58.850
it will happen in real life.

00:57:58.850 --> 00:58:01.812
And that's why I'm
so focused laser

00:58:01.812 --> 00:58:05.524
like on onscreen depictions.

00:58:05.524 --> 00:58:08.193
And I mean, we
know things like--

00:58:08.193 --> 00:58:11.988
it's just that media images
have an incredible power

00:58:11.988 --> 00:58:13.907
to normalize things.

00:58:13.907 --> 00:58:17.911
And we know that there are
so many more women than men

00:58:17.911 --> 00:58:21.206
now in forensic science,
for example, because of CSI.

00:58:21.206 --> 00:58:24.459
Women saw it on TV and
said, wow, I could do that.

00:58:24.459 --> 00:58:26.878
Another interesting
thing was in 2012

00:58:26.878 --> 00:58:30.882
after Hunger Games and
Brave both came out,

00:58:30.882 --> 00:58:34.344
girls' participation
in archery skyrocketed

00:58:34.344 --> 00:58:36.346
to be the most
populated category

00:58:36.346 --> 00:58:38.223
of competitive archery,
because they saw it.

00:58:38.223 --> 00:58:42.436
And they said, oh wow, I
guess girls can do that.

00:58:42.436 --> 00:58:45.605
Also when I got to play
the president on TV--

00:58:45.605 --> 00:58:49.693
I had very brief
administration sadly,

00:58:49.693 --> 00:58:55.991
but research found that after
the show had been on people

00:58:55.991 --> 00:58:59.411
were 58% more likely to
vote for a female candidate

00:58:59.411 --> 00:59:00.787
for president.

00:59:00.787 --> 00:59:04.207
And so we really do
need to take advantage

00:59:04.207 --> 00:59:08.712
of the power of these images
to transform the world.

00:59:08.712 --> 00:59:11.465
And I would say, maybe
because it's the rooms I'm in,

00:59:11.465 --> 00:59:14.342
we unfortunately have an
ability as the deciders,

00:59:14.342 --> 00:59:17.137
decision makers
for these projects,

00:59:17.137 --> 00:59:20.307
you could talk about let's say
we're doing an Israeli project.

00:59:20.307 --> 00:59:23.435
There's still a weird problem
where you don't look around

00:59:23.435 --> 00:59:26.062
the room and go, wait a minute.

00:59:26.062 --> 00:59:26.855
I'm not Israeli.

00:59:26.855 --> 00:59:28.523
I need Israeli
people in the room.

00:59:28.523 --> 00:59:31.860
And it's got to be as crazy
as when we see cigarettes

00:59:31.860 --> 00:59:34.196
in movies now that
just look insane,

00:59:34.196 --> 00:59:35.405
it's got to feel that weird.

00:59:35.405 --> 00:59:37.574
If you're in a room you
have to have inclusion.

00:59:37.574 --> 00:59:39.493
So I don't know, I
want systemic inclusion

00:59:39.493 --> 00:59:43.121
where that's just a normal thing
that anybody would feel insane

00:59:43.121 --> 00:59:46.875
talking about something without
that person in the room.

00:59:46.875 --> 00:59:49.961
And I also have hope, because
I think the kids get it.

00:59:49.961 --> 00:59:52.380
And they want it and
are demanding it,

00:59:52.380 --> 00:59:56.718
and we need to just give them
power to help us through this.

00:59:56.718 --> 00:59:59.262
And your hope, Geena?

00:59:59.262 --> 01:00:05.227
Oh, my hope is that we can
make this change happen fast.

01:00:05.227 --> 01:00:09.815
You know, that in the time it
takes to create a new TV show

01:00:09.815 --> 01:00:11.650
or create a new
movie, we can change

01:00:11.650 --> 01:00:12.943
what the future looks like.

01:00:12.943 --> 01:00:16.613
You know, we don't have to
sneak up on this problem.

01:00:16.613 --> 01:00:20.826
We can make wholesale
change right away,

01:00:20.826 --> 01:00:21.785
particularly on screen.

01:00:21.785 --> 01:00:23.328
The easiest place to do it.

01:00:23.328 --> 01:00:27.123
And things will
change if we do that.

01:00:27.123 --> 01:00:28.041
That's great.

01:00:28.041 --> 01:00:31.503
Well, let me just say I have
hope because of all of you,

01:00:31.503 --> 01:00:33.505
and the work that you're doing.

01:00:33.505 --> 01:00:36.174
I have spent the entire
weekend really thinking

01:00:36.174 --> 01:00:41.221
about the court, and the
loss that we are experiencing

01:00:41.221 --> 01:00:45.475
right now in terms of Ruth
Bader Ginsburg and her absence.

01:00:45.475 --> 01:00:51.147
And the absence of her voice,
her aperture, from the court.

01:00:51.147 --> 01:00:53.859
But what you give me
is hope that change

01:00:53.859 --> 01:00:57.612
is happening in other places.

01:00:57.612 --> 01:01:00.532
And I believe that it has
to happen in all places.

01:01:00.532 --> 01:01:04.870
It can't just take place in
one part of the government,

01:01:04.870 --> 01:01:07.622
or one part of industry.

01:01:07.622 --> 01:01:11.042
And I don't think
there's any industry that

01:01:11.042 --> 01:01:14.254
has the influence
and the power to make

01:01:14.254 --> 01:01:18.842
it happen in other places than
in the entertainment industry.

01:01:18.842 --> 01:01:23.597
So I am hopeful that gender
equity will be a reality,

01:01:23.597 --> 01:01:26.892
and I know that you will
all be contributing to it.

01:01:26.892 --> 01:01:30.687
So thank you, and thanks
for this conversation.

01:01:30.687 --> 01:01:31.938
Thank you.

01:01:31.938 --> 01:01:33.231
Thanks, everybody.

01:01:33.231 --> 01:01:34.858
Hi, I'm Geena Davis.

01:01:34.858 --> 01:01:38.111
Thank you for watching
Academy Dialogues.

